    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/pelagius-of-asturias"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pelagius-of-asturias.jpg" alt="Pelagius of Asturias" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/pelagius-of-asturias">Pelagius of Asturias</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pelayo_en_la_batalla_de_Covadonga_BNE_Mss_2805_f_23r.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AnonymousUnknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asturias">Asturias</a> arose in the Cantabrian Mountains of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> a few years after the Umayyad conquest of the Visigothic Kingdom, which began in 711. According to the Asturian chronicles of the late ninth century, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pelagius-of-asturias">Pelagius</a>, or Pelayo, a Visigothic noble who may have served in the royal guard, was chosen as leader by the Asturians and led a revolt against the Muslim governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munuza">Munuza</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gijon">Gijón</a>. His followers defeated a Muslim force in the valley of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-covadonga">Covadonga</a>, an event dated traditionally to 718 or, by many historians, to 722. The chronicles turned this skirmish into a miraculous victory, and its scale is uncertain, but Muslim authorities did not afterwards control the mountains. Pelagius ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cangas-de-onis">Cangas de Onís</a> until his death in 737; his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/favila-of-asturias">Favila</a> was killed by a bear in 739. The throne then passed to Alfonso I, son of Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-of-cantabria">Peter of Cantabria</a> and husband of Pelagius’s daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ermesinda">Ermesinda</a>, which united <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asturia">Asturias</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cantabria">Cantabria</a>. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> garrisons of the north-west rebelled and withdrew in the 740s, Alfonso raided the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/douro">Douro</a> valley, took towns such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucus-augusti">Lugo</a>, León and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astorga">Astorga</a>, and brought their Christian inhabitants north. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fruela-i-of-asturias">Fruela I</a> founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oviedo">Oviedo</a> around 761, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-ii-of-asturias">Alfonso II</a> later made it the permanent royal seat.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/oviedo"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oviedo.jpg" alt="Oviedo" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/oviedo">Oviedo</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Catedral_de_Oviedo_03.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Simon Burchell, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asturias">Asturias</a> was a poor, rural and mountainous kingdom. Most of its people were free peasants and herdsmen living in small villages in the valleys between the Cantabrian Mountains and the sea, raising <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, sheep and pigs and growing cereals, apples and vines where the climate allowed. Towns were few and small; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oviedo">Oviedo</a> grew around its churches and palace rather than as a centre of trade, and coin was scarce, so that land, livestock and cloth often served as measures of value. Monasteries were numerous and became important landowners and agents of settlement. From the ninth century the kingdom expanded southwards through organised resettlement, in which peasants, monasteries and nobles took possession of abandoned land by the practice of <em>presura</em>, while Christians from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a>, later called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozarabs">Mozarabs</a>, migrated north and brought churchmen, books and building traditions. The population mixed Asturians, Cantabrians, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicians">Galicians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basques">Basques</a> and refugees of Hispano-Gothic descent. Religious life was vigorous: the monk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beatus-of-liebana">Beatus of Liébana</a> wrote his influential <em>Commentary on the Apocalypse</em> around 776 and attacked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adoptionism">Adoptionist</a> teaching of Archbishop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elipandus">Elipandus of Toledo</a>, and the discovery of what was believed to be the tomb of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-the-great">Saint James</a> the Apostle at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago-de-compostela">Compostela</a> in the early ninth century began a cult that drew pilgrims from across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-christendom">Latin Europe</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-albelda"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-albelda.jpg" alt="Battle of Albelda" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-albelda">Battle of Albelda</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Monte_Laturce.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">BigSus, CC BY 2.5</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Asturian armies were small forces of nobles and their followers, free peasants and mountain infantry, who relied on the rugged terrain, fortified sites and raids rather than on pitched battles. For much of the eighth and ninth centuries the kingdom was on the defensive against the summer expeditions sent from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>. Armies of the emir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hisham-i-of-cordoba">Hisham I</a> sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oviedo">Oviedo</a> in 794 and 795, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-ii-of-asturias">Alfonso II</a> defeated one Muslim force at Lutos in 794, and in 798 he raided as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a>, sending captured booty to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramiro-garces-of-viguera">Ramiro</a> I repelled a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> raid on the coast in 844, while his son Ordoño I defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musa-ibn-musa">Musa ibn Musa</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-qasi">Banu Qasi</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-albelda">Albelda</a> in 859, according to the chronicles, and began to resettle León and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astorga">Astorga</a>. The later legend of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-clavijo">Battle of Clavijo</a>, in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-the-great">Saint James</a> supposedly appeared to fight for Ramiro I, has no historical basis. The greatest advances came under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-iii-of-asturias">Alfonso III the Great</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vimara-peres">Vímara Peres</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porto">Porto</a> in 868, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hermenegildo-gutierrez">Hermenegildo Guterres</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coimbra">Coimbra</a> in 878, and in the same year Alfonso’s forces defeated a Córdoban army at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-polvoraria">Polvoraria</a>. Fortified towns at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamora">Zamora</a>, from 893, Simancas, Toro and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgos">Burgos</a> anchored a frontier along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/douro">Douro</a>, and the eastern march became known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>, the land of castles.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/chronicle-of-albelda"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chronicle-of-albelda.jpg" alt="Chronicle of Albelda" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/chronicle-of-albelda">Chronicle of Albelda</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Scribes_Codex_Vigilanus.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Pertenece al Codex Vigilanus (folio 428) su probable autor sea el escriba y monje Vigila o Vigilán. (by Rockger21), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Asturian monarchy was small and personal. Kings were chosen from among the kin of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pelagius-of-asturias">Pelagius</a> and Alfonso I, and successions were often disputed: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fruela-i-of-asturias">Fruela I</a> was murdered in 768, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-ii-of-asturias">Alfonso II</a> was briefly overthrown around 801 before his supporters restored him. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quintus-poppaedius-silo">Silo</a> moved the court to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pravia">Pravia</a>, but under Alfonso II the Chaste (791–842) <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oviedo">Oviedo</a> became a true capital, with a palace, a cathedral and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chronicle-of-albelda">Chronicle of Albelda</a> claims that Alfonso II restored in Oviedo the whole order of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a>, as it had been in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a>, in church and palace, and court officials, counts and bishops modelled on Visigothic practice appear in the sources. The Visigothic law code, the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigothic-code">Liber Iudiciorum</a></em>, continued to be cited in legal disputes. Royal power was exercised through counts who governed districts such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>, Álava and, after 868, Portugal, often as powerful local lords with their own retinues. The church was closely tied to the crown, and kings endowed monasteries and cathedrals to secure prayers, loyalty and settlement of new land. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-iii-of-asturias">Alfonso III</a> (866–910), the court sponsored historical writing, notably the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chronicle-of-alfonso-iii">Chronicle of Alfonso III</a> and the Chronicle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-albelda">Albelda</a>, which presented the Asturian kings as heirs of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigoths">Visigoths</a> destined to recover the whole of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/san-julian-de-los-prados"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/san-julian-de-los-prados.jpg" alt="San Julián de los Prados" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/san-julian-de-los-prados">San Julián de los Prados</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Facade_of_the_church_of_San_Juli%C3%A1n_de_los_Prados_-_Oviedo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jaume Meneses, CC BY-SA 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asturias">Asturias</a> was the seed from which the Christian kingdoms of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a> grew. The Kingdom of León was its direct continuation, and the counties of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> and Portugal first appear as frontier districts of the Asturian crown; both later became kingdoms that dominated the peninsula. The ideology of its chroniclers, that the Asturian kings were legitimate heirs of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigoths">Visigoths</a> with a duty to restore Christian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, became a founding idea of the medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reconquista">Reconquista</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-covadonga">Covadonga</a> became a national symbol of Spanish history. The cult of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-the-great">Saint James</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago-de-compostela">Compostela</a>, promoted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-ii-of-asturias">Alfonso II</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-iii-of-asturias">Alfonso III</a>, grew into one of the three great pilgrimages of medieval Christendom, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camino-de-santiago">Way of Saint James</a> carried people, money and ideas from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and beyond into northern Spain. The kingdom produced a distinctive pre-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanesque-architecture">Romanesque architecture</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-julian-de-los-prados">San Julián de los Prados</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oviedo">Oviedo</a> and the hall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santa-maria-del-naranco">Santa María del Naranco</a> and the church of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-miguel-de-lillo">San Miguel de Lillo</a> built under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramiro-garces-of-viguera">Ramiro</a> I, together with metalwork such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cross-of-the-angels">Cross of the Angels</a> of 808 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cross-of-victory">Cross of Victory</a> of 908. These monuments are listed as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>. The heir to the Spanish crown still bears the title of Prince or Princess of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asturia">Asturias</a>, created in 1388.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-iii-of-asturias"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alfonso-iii-of-asturias.jpg" alt="Alfonso III of Asturias" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-iii-of-asturias">Alfonso III of Asturias</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Testamento.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Pelagius, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asturias">Asturias</a> did not fall to an enemy; it was transformed by its own success. As <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-iii-of-asturias">Alfonso III</a> pushed the frontier to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/douro">Douro</a>, the political centre of the kingdom moved south from the mountains to the plains of León, which offered more land, better communications and a base close to the frontier. In 910 Alfonso III was forced to give up power by his sons, apparently with the support of leading nobles and of his wife, Queen Jimena; he died the same year at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamora">Zamora</a>. The realm was divided among his sons: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garcia-i-of-leon">García I</a> took León, the new centre of royal power, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ordono-ii-of-leon">Ordoño II</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fruela-ii-of-asturias">Fruela II</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asturia">Asturias</a> itself. García died in 914 and Ordoño II succeeded him in León, keeping Galicia, so that León became the principal kingdom and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oviedo">Oviedo</a> a provincial seat. When Ordoño II died in 924, Fruela II succeeded him as king of León, reuniting the inheritance under the crown of León. From this point historians speak of the Kingdom of León as the successor of Asturias. Oviedo remained an important religious centre, with its cathedral and royal pantheon, and Asturias kept its identity as the cradle of the Christian monarchy, but the kingdom itself had ceased to exist as a separate state.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asturias">Asturias</a> was the first Christian state to emerge in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a> after the Muslim conquest of 711, and from its mountain base it grew into the Kingdom of León, founded the pilgrimage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago-de-compostela">Compostela</a> and created the idea of Christian recovery that shaped medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and Portugal.</em></p>

